### ZR/ZF scope correction

The independently audited ZR/ZF theorem is a theorem about the **nonisolated positive-contact core** `V`, not the full source. With `v=|V|`,

\[
\ker\Omega_V=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1,P_x|_V,P_y|_V\},
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}[V]=2v-3,
\]

and therefore

\[
e+h\ge2v-2,
\qquad
h\ge\delta\ge\lceil D\rceil.
\]

Contact-isolated source points remain in the global configuration. Extending the core stress matrix by zero rows and columns creates additional kernel directions. `C-ZRF-FULL-SOURCE` remains rejected. No bound on the isolated source population follows from ZR/ZF.

### Current C-II frontier

Audit.62 controls theorem authority.

All audit.52--61 results remain canonical. Audit.61 reduces a sufficiently small O--O finite-motion failure to
\[
\boxed{
\text{outer-face near-unit owner/implication structure}
+\text{explicit unary rhombus blockers}
+\text{at most }d_H\text{ inactive upper exceptions}.
}
\]

Audit.62 adds two finite geometry constraints.

- \`C-LOADED-NEAR-UNIT-ADJACENCY\`: for the actual loaded diameter-endpoint set \(B\),
  \[
  \tau_D=\frac{3D}{\sqrt{3D^2+4}+1},
  \]
  and every nonconsecutive loaded pair \(a,b\) with \(|ab|<D\) satisfies
  \[
  |ab|\ge\tau_D.
  \]
  In particular, for \(D\ge6\), every loaded pair of length at most \(13/12\) is a loaded-hull side. The loaded near-unit graph is therefore a subgraph of the loaded hull cycle and has maximum degree at most two.
- \`C-LOADED-HULL-CAP-BUDGET\`: with
  \[
  K=\operatorname{conv}A,\qquad
  F=\operatorname{conv}B,\qquad
  W=\operatorname{per}K-q,
  \]
  occupied loaded-hull caps are at most \(W\), their total cap arc/base length is at most \(2W\), short core-to-exterior attachments are supported on at most \(68W\) core vertices, the nonloaded \(1/2\)-boundary layer inside \(F\) has size at most \(12W\), every deeper point has exact upper margin \(D-1/2\), and
  \[
  \#(\operatorname{ext}K\setminus B)\le W.
  \]
  Since canonical full-source geometry gives \(\operatorname{per}K<\pi D\), every canonical subsequence with \(q/D\to\pi\) has \(W=o(D)\). Hence
  \[
  |P|=o(D),\qquad |X|=o(D),\qquad
  \#(\operatorname{ext}K\setminus B)=o(D).
  \]

### Exact remaining split

1. **Full-core branch.** The lower finite-motion obstruction is still an outer-face owner/implication problem, but audit.62 removes two major hiding places. Loaded-loaded near-unit clauses are cyclically ordered on the actual loaded hull, and terminal hull-cap/exterior blocker populations are sublinear. Any linear obstruction in a \(q/D\to\pi\) subsequence must therefore concentrate on actual outer-contact-face geometry inside the loaded hull, the cyclic loaded-hull near-unit graph, explicit unary rhombus blockers, or the at most \(d_H\) inactive upper exceptions.

   The highest-value next theorem is an **outer-face owner-order theorem** using loaded-hull cyclic order, audit.53 loaded-return paths, audit.56 active-diameter coordinates, and audit.60 forcing-path structure to rule out owner contradiction cycles/paths or construct a satisfying shortening assignment.

2. **Macroscopic-container branch.** Unchanged. Audit.62 is finite source geometry and does not alter the separate inactive-container route.

The audit.61 regressions remain binding: source-level path forests may become cyclic after owner contraction, and unary rhombus blockers cannot be discarded merely because motion amplitude is small.

